#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163251 :tutorial/content "A discrete random variable $X$ follows a **discrete uniform distribution** over a set $S$ if each value in $S$ is equally likely.\n\nThe probability mass function for the discrete uniform distribution on a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{\\begin{vmatrix}S &},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ S,; 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over the set\n\n$$\nS=\\{s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\}\n$$\n\nwe denote it as\n\n$$\nX \\sim U \\left\\{s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nand we say that $X$ is **uniformly distributed** over $S$.\n\nFor example, consider the random variable $T\\sim U\\{1,2,4\\}$. In this case,\n\n$$\nS = \\{1,2,4\\}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mid S \\mid = 3\n$$\n\nand therefore the probability mass function for $T$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}t ∈ {1, 2, 4}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nA sketch of $f(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cbac12f164635cd4efaa94167a4582689d470c3373769e1aa9facc2670f732ef.png)\n\n**Note:** In this lesson, we will make use of the following formula for computing the cardinality of a set of consecutive numbers:\n\n$$\n\\left| \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b \\} \\right| = b - a + 1\n$$\n\nDon't forget about the $+1$ at the end of the formula. The $+1$ is needed because we are including both endpoints $a$ and $b$ in the count. (Not convinced? Notice that\n\n$$\n\\left| \\{4, 5 \\} \\right| = 5 - 4 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\n)" :tutorial/id #uuid "09eacd15-bdd6-56f7-a6b3-5f9659e21543" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
